{"causes": ["The collapse of central authority during the late Eastern Han dynasty created a power vacuum filled by ambitious regional warlords.", "Cao Cao's military campaigns to consolidate power in the Central Plains led to his confrontation with Zhang Xiu at Wan City in 197 CE.", "Cao Cao's personal indiscretion with Zhang Xiu's aunt provoked Zhang Xiu into a sudden and violent revolt against the unsuspecting Cao army."], "effects": ["Cao Anmin's death contributed to a significant personal and strategic setback for Cao Cao, highlighting the dangers of military overconfidence.", "The tragic loss of Cao Anmin, Cao Ang, and Dian Wei deepened Cao Cao's resolve and possibly influenced his later strategic ruthlessness.", "The Battle of Wan City became a cautionary tale within Cao Cao's history, emphasizing the high cost of betrayal and the unpredictability of war."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "cao-cao", "sourceName": "Cao Cao", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "cao-anmin", "targetName": "Cao Anmin", "context": "Cao Anmin's identity and military service were defined by his familial relationship and loyalty to his uncle, Cao Cao, a prominent warlord of the late Han dynasty."}, {"sourceSlug": "cao-anmin", "sourceName": "Cao Anmin", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "wan-cheng-china", "targetName": "Wan City, China", "context": "Cao Anmin met his end during the Battle of Wan City in 197 CE, where he sacrificed himself to save Cao Cao."}, {"sourceSlug": "cao-anmin", "sourceName": "Cao Anmin", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "battle-of-wan-city-197-ce", "targetName": "Battle of Wan City (197 CE)", "context": "Cao Anmin was a direct participant in the Battle of Wan City in 197 CE, where he perished during Zhang Xiu's surprise attack."}, {"sourceSlug": "cao-anmin", "sourceName": "Cao Anmin", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "cao-cao-personal-grief-and-strategic-caution", "targetName": "Cao Cao's Personal Grief and Strategic Caution", "context": "Cao Anmin's death, alongside Cao Ang and Dian Wei, caused profound grief for Cao Cao and contributed to his later strategic caution and ruthlessness."}, {"sourceSlug": "zhang-xiu", "sourceName": "Zhang Xiu", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "cao-anmin", "targetName": "Cao Anmin", "context": "Zhang Xiu's surprise revolt and counterattack at Wan City in 197 CE directly led to Cao Anmin's death in battle."}], "places": [{"name": "Wan City, China", "role": "Site of death"}, {"name": "Xuchang, China", "role": "Cao Cao's capital"}, {"name": "Central Plains, China", "role": "Region of conflict"}], "texts": [], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q702587"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Cao Anmin's death at the Battle of Wan City in 197 CE, though not a direct act of leadership, served as a crucial catalyst for Cao Cao's personal grief and strategic adjustments, indirectly shaping the trajectory of the Cao Wei state and the Three Kingdoms period. His sacrifice became a poignant symbol of the high personal costs of the era's conflicts.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:23:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42971253367", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Cao Anmin (b. 200 CE, China) was a public servant or civil administrator of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in China. Known in the fields of military leaders & commanders.", "newValue": "Cao Anmin (d. 197 CE) was a pivotal, though tragic, figure in the early stages of the Three Kingdoms period in ancient China, serving as a nephew to the formidable warlord Cao Cao. His untimely death at the Battle of Wan City in 197 CE, during an unexpected revolt by Zhang Xiu, profoundly impacted Cao Cao's personal life and strategic decisions, setting a precedent for the high costs of betrayal and military miscalculation within the burgeoning power struggles of the late Eastern Han dynasty. Anmin's fate is intrinsically linked to the consolidation of Cao Cao's power base in the Central Plains and the subsequent establishment of the state of Cao Wei.

Cao Anmin's primary, albeit brief, moment in historical record is his involvement in Cao Cao's campaign against Zhang Xiu in 197 CE. After Zhang Xiu's initial surrender at Wan City, Cao Cao's inappropriate liaison with Zhang Xiu's aunt incited a furious counterattack. During the ensuing chaos, Cao Anmin bravely gave his horse to his uncle, Cao Cao, enabling the warlord's escape, but sacrificing his own life in the process. His loyalty and self-sacrifice, alongside the deaths of Cao Cao's eldest son Cao Ang and the general Dian Wei, underscored the volatile nature of military campaigns and the personal sacrifices demanded during this era of fragmentation.

The death of Cao Anmin, particularly alongside Cao Ang, left a deep and lasting psychological wound on Cao Cao, who later expressed profound regret for the circumstances that led to their demise. This event is often cited as a critical turning point in Cao Cao's personal narrative, influencing his future ruthlessness and strategic caution. It also contributed to the narrative of Cao Cao as a leader who, despite his genius, was prone to personal failings that could have devastating consequences for his family and trusted lieutenants, thereby shaping the complex perception of his character for subsequent generations."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:23:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42971253367", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Classical era: flourishing of philosophical inquiry, imperial expansion, and codification of la", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "200 CE, China"}, {"title": "Military conflicts and strategic necessities", "type": "Idea", "year": "200 CE, China"}], "newValue": ["The collapse of central authority during the late Eastern Han dynasty created a power vacuum filled by ambitious regional warlords.", "Cao Cao's military campaigns to consolidate power in the Central Plains led to his confrontation with Zhang Xiu at Wan City in 197 CE.", "Cao Cao's personal indiscretion with Zhang Xiu's aunt provoked Zhang Xiu into a sudden and violent revolt against the unsuspecting Cao army."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-11T06:23:04Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#42971253367", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Determined military outcomes shaping geopolitical boundaries", "type": "Idea", "year": "197 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to enduring intellectual and institutional frameworks shaping subsequent civilizati", "type": "Movement", "year": "197 CE"}], "newValue": ["Cao Anmin's death contributed to a significant personal and strategic setback for Cao Cao, highlighting the dangers of military overconfiden